# 5 entries, one genuinely distinct fact per line.
colonization | definition | Spanish rule over the archipelago ran from 1565 to 1898, and it is the period that most shaped modern Filipino language and culture. Spanish was the official language of government and education, which is why many Filipino words are Spanish in origin. It ended after the 1896 revolution and the 1898 Spanish-American War. | history, spanish, colony, colonize, manila | philippine history, history of the philippines, spanish colonial philippines | pilipinas | pilipinas | Ang Pilipinas ay isang kapuluang bansa sa Timog-Silangang Asya, binubuo ng higit sa 7,600 pulo, mga isang libo lamang ang nakatira. Nasa kanlurang Pasipiko ito, sa sona ng mga bagyong tropics, kaya kadalasang tinatamaan. Si Manila ang kabataang bansa, at hatiin ito sa mga rehiyon at saka mga probinsiya - mas malaki ang Quezon City kaysa sa mismong kapital, na nalilito ang mga tao sa palagay na ang kabataan ay pinakamalaking lungsod. Maraming talagang wika ang Pilipinas: opisyal ang Filipino at Ingles, at sa tahanan ang karamihan ng pamilya ay Cebuano, Tagalog, Ilocano o Hiligayno, madalas marami sa iisang usapan. | Ang Pilipinas archipelago siya sa Southeast Asia, nga binuo gikan sa labaw pa sa 7,600 nga isla, nga mga usa ka libo lang nga populated. Naa siya sa western Pacific, sa belt nga tropical cyclones, kana man nga gyud nga nahit nga kanunay. Ang Manila ang capital, ug naa nga mga region ang nasud sa country, dayon mga probinsiya - ang Quezon City gyud nga mas dako kaysa sa capital mismo, mao nga niini nahimong ambag sa mga tawo nga akong kana ang capital mao pud ang pinakadako nga syudad. Maraming languages gyud ang Pilipinas: official ang Filipino ug English, ug sa balay ang daghan ka mga pamilya mag-Cebuano, Tagalog, Ilocano o Hiligaynon, kanunay daghan niini sa usa ka conversation. | General geography. Island count varies by counting convention; treat as approximate.
commonwealth | definition | American administration lasted from 1898 until 1946. It brought a large English-language education system, which later became the language of government and business, and it promised independence, which was delayed. The Commonwealth of 1935 was a transitional step, and full independence came on 4 July 1946. | philippines, edoardo, quinto, commonwealth, independence | american period philippines, when did the philippines get independence |  |
japanese occupation | definition | The Japanese occupation of the Philippines lasted from 1942 to 1945, and it is the period the Philippines remembers most sharply because the fighting reached ordinary civilians rather than only soldiers. The invasion began with the bombing of Manila and other cities shortly after Pearl Harbor in December 1941, which destroyed much of the capital while the defenders were severely outnumbered, and the subsequent three-year campaign was fought largely by guerrillas because the Japanese held the towns and the roads while losing elsewhere. The retaliation in the provinces was severe and included the Bataan Death March, and a whole generation of women is known for the assault during that period. There were three major administrations under the occupation: Emilio Aguinaldo headed the second republic, Jose Laurel headed the Japanese-sponsored republic, and the resistance was led from the United States by the Commonwealth government under Manuel Roxas. The end came with the atomic bombings and the American landings, and independence was restored on 4 July 1946, now remembered as Philippine Independence Day. | 1942, 1945, bataan, guerrilla, manila, aguinaldo, laurel | what happened during the japanese occupation, when did the japanese occupy the philippines, what is the bataan death march | hanggang panahon | japanese occupation

occupation | definition | Japan occupied the Philippines from 1942 to 1945, one of the earliest moves of the Second World War. The occupation included the Bataan Death March and the bombing of Manila, which destroyed much of the city. The Philippines fought alongside the Allies, and the damage is remembered as a defining wound of the war. | japan, occupation, 1942, manila | world war, second world war, japanese occupation philippines, world war 2 philippines |  |
bar revolution | definition | The Philippine Revolution of 1896 was a revolt against Spanish colonial rule, and it is usually dated to August 1896 when the Katipunan organisation rose in Bulacan, which is why a Bulacan town is often described as the birthplace of the revolution. The immediate cause was an alleged conspiracy by three priests being returned to Bishop Blanco, which Spanish authorities mishandled and which turned into a mass uprising; the broader cause was a decade of economic pressure, the ratchet tax and the Lancaster Law, and of Catholic clergy being pushed out of public life. The Katipunan had been founded in 1892 and its leadership, Bonifacio and the Luna brothers, gave the movement its name and its emblem. It did not succeed militarily against a well-supplied colonial power, and the capture of Malolos and the Republic in 1899 were followed by a shift of power to the Americans after a short war. Its significance is less a victory than a claim: that Filipinos had organised and declared a national government, which every later independence movement inherited. | katipunan, 1896, bulacan, malolos, colonialism, revolt, declaration | what was the philippine revolution, when did the philippine revolution start, what is the katipunan | rebolusyon | bar revolution

katipunan | definition | The Katipunan was a secret society founded by Andres Bonifacio in 1892 to end Spanish rule. Its members, the Katipuneros, came from across the social classes - workers, farmers and students. On 12 August 1896 members tore their cedulas and burned them in Balintawak, which became the start of the Philippine Revolution. | philippines, declamation, revolution, 1896, bonifacio | declaration of independence, katipunan, andres bonifacio |  |
cession | definition | Under the Treaty of Paris of December 1898, Spain surrendered the Philippines to the United States, alongside Puerto Rico and Guam. The terms were worked out in Paris while fighting was still happening in the Philippines, and the resulting annexation was strongly opposed by Aguinaldo and the revolutionary government. | paris, cession, spain | treaty of paris, treaty of paris 1898, how did spain lose the philippines |  |
